course: dictionary
lesson: 12183
title: Condiments
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A condiment is something you add to food to give it more flavour. Salt, pepper, ketchup, mustard and vinegar are all condiments.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «She shook some salt onto her chips.» Salt is added to the chips after they are cooked, so salt is a condiment. Ask yourself: is this something you add to food to make it taste better? If yes, it is a condiment.
explain: A condiment is not the main meal. It is added to food, like pepper on an egg or mustard on a sausage. «The table was set with ketchup, mustard and vinegar.» Those are condiments, ready for people to use.
fact: A condiment is a sauce, spice or other thing that you add to food to give it more flavour.
fact: Common condiments include salt, pepper, ketchup, mustard and vinegar.
fact: «Condiments» is the plural. One condiment, two or more condiments.
fact: Restaurants and cafés often put condiments on the table so that you can add your own.
fact: In science lessons about taste, condiments can add salty, sour or spicy flavours to food.
exercise: What is a condiment? | A cold drink served with ice | *Something you add to food to give it more flavour | A plate or bowl you eat from | A main meal that you cook in a pan
exercise: Which word is a condiment? | Spoon | Table | Plate | *Ketchup
exercise: «The café has condiments on every table.» Which word in the sentence is the plural of condiment? | every | *condiments | café | table
test: Which of these is NOT a condiment? | Pepper | *Bread | Mustard | Vinegar
test: Sam says, «Pass the condiments, please.» What does Sam want? | To take the plates away | *The sauces and spices to add to the food | To leave the café | To be given a drink
exam: «Mum added a dash of vinegar to the salad to make it sour and tangy.» Why did Mum add the vinegar? | To wash the bowl | *To give the salad more flavour | To cook the salad in a pan | To make the salad cold
